Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 07:23:20 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Keep gcc's format-overflow findings non-fatal in host builds

gcc treats -Wformat-overflow as part of -Werror=format, so the host
build on Windows (MinGW gcc 15) stopped in gadget.c on fixed-size
sprintf buffers.  Those findings predate the port and are not the
%ld class -Werror=format is there for.  Under gcc they are now plain
warnings; clang builds are unchanged.
---
 src/make/Makefile-host | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/make/Makefile-host b/src/make/Makefile-host
index 75b10a6..27088a4 100644
--- a/src/make/Makefile-host
+++ b/src/make/Makefile-host
@@ -74,6 +74,13 @@ CFLAGS ?= -O2
 CFLAGS += -std=gnu89 -DACE_HOST -Wformat -Werror=format \
           -Wno-deprecated-non-prototype
 
+# gcc (not clang) counts -Wformat-overflow as part of -Werror=format.  Its
+# findings (fixed-size sprintf buffers in gadget.c) predate the port and are
+# not the %ld class guarded above, so gcc reports them as warnings only.
+ifeq ($(findstring clang,$(shell $(CC) --version 2>/dev/null)),)
+  CFLAGS += -Wno-error=format-overflow
+endif
+
 # WARN=1 turns on -Wall.  Off by default: these are 1990s K&R sources and the
 # ~150 unused-variable/dangling-else findings are pre-existing noise that would
 # bury anything new.
